*** SPOILER ALERT ***








If the tin is cursed, the meat will always be rotten.  Perhaps your tinning
kit is cursed - that would produce only cursed tins. 

For normal and blessed tins of monster meat, there is an equal chance that
the meat will be:

  1) Deep Fried - Causing slippery fingers, you'll drop your weapon and
      rings.  This can be a disaster if you are wielding a crysknife,
      depending on Fire Brand in hell, or standing over a sink!

  2) Pickled - not very nourishing

  3) Soup - even less nourishing

  4) Pureed - nourishing!

  5) Rotten - same as if the tin were cursed

Blessed tins open faster.  Of course if the tin is spinach, none of the
above applies.

Commentary: A tinning kit is most useful early in the game before poison
resistance is acquired.  It allows you to tin killer bees and scorpions
and then eat the tinned meat in an attempt to gain poison resistance without
losing STR in the attempt.  Once I have a unicorn horn, I'll drop the
tinning kit because its too damn heavy.  If you do keep it, you can use it
to tin trolls to prevent them from annoying you, and to tin dragon meat.
Since I've generally got teleport and control by the time I hit the dragons,
if I kill one that I want to eat for an intrinsic, I just burn off food
by teleporting, til its safe to eat the corpse.  Oh yes, if I miscalculate
and the corpse is tainted, the unicorn horn will bail me out.

P.S. I did tin the Wizard of Yendor once to see if it would stop him from
resurrecting.  It didn't help.


